Nice question 👍 annotations in Ingress are one of the most common real-world use cases in Kubernetes. Let’s go step by step and keep it beginner-friendly.

🌱 What is Ingress in Kubernetes?
Ingress is like the main gate of your Kubernetes cluster.

It controls how external traffic (from the internet) reaches your internal services (Pods).

Example: If someone visits myapp.example.com, Ingress knows which Pod should handle it.

But… Ingress itself is just rules.
To make it work, you need an Ingress Controller (like NGINX, HAProxy, AWS ALB controller, etc.).

🌱 Where do Annotations Come In?
Annotations are extra instructions you give to the Ingress Controller (not to Kubernetes itself).

Kubernetes does not process Ingress annotations.

Instead, the Ingress Controller reads them and configures itself accordingly.

👉 Think of it like leaving sticky notes for the Ingress Controller saying:
“Hey, when traffic comes here, please rewrite the path”
or
“Force everyone to use HTTPS.”

🔹 Example Ingress with Annotations (NGINX Controller)
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: Ingress
metadata:
  name: myapp-ingress
  annotations:
    nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/rewrite-target: /
    nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/ssl-redirect: "true"
spec:
  rules:
  - host: myapp.example.com
    http:
      paths:
      - path: /app
        pathType: Prefix
        backend:
          service:
            name: myapp-service
            port:
              number: 80
🔎 Explanation (How These Annotations Work Internally)
You apply this Ingress manifest → Kubernetes stores it in etcd as an object.

Ingress Controller (e.g., NGINX Ingress Controller) is running in your cluster as a Pod.

It constantly watches the API server for changes in Ingress objects.

When it sees your Ingress, it reads the annotations:

nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/rewrite-target: / → tells NGINX to strip /app from the URL and forward only / to the backend.

nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/ssl-redirect: "true" → tells NGINX to automatically redirect http:// requests to https://.

The Ingress Controller updates its internal NGINX config.

If you kubectl exec into the NGINX Ingress Controller Pod, you’ll see these annotations reflected in its NGINX config.

Now, when a user opens http://myapp.example.com/app →

NGINX sees /app, rewrites it to /, and sends traffic to the Pod’s Service.

If the user uses HTTP, it redirects to HTTPS.

🔹 Other Common Annotations in Ingress
Force HTTPS:

nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/ssl-redirect: "true"
Set request timeout:

nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/proxy-connect-timeout: "10s"
Limit request body size (file uploads):

nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/proxy-body-size: "20m"
Custom error pages:

nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/custom-http-errors: "404,503"
🎯 Simple Analogy
Ingress object = “traffic rulebook” (says where traffic should go).

Ingress Controller = “security guard at the gate” (applies the rules).

Annotations = “special instructions to the guard” (like check ID cards, force HTTPS, limit baggage size).

✅ Summary

Annotations in Ingress are not used by Kubernetes itself.

They are read by the Ingress Controller (e.g., NGINX, HAProxy, AWS ALB controller).

They tell the controller how to handle traffic (rewrite URLs, enable HTTPS, set timeouts, etc.).

