🔹 Example Ingress
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: Ingress
metadata:
  name: myapp-ingress
  annotations:
    nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/rewrite-target: /
spec:
  rules:
  - host: myapp.example.com
    http:
      paths:
      - path: /app
        pathType: Prefix
        backend:
          service:
            name: myapp-service
            port:
              number: 80
🔎 How It Works
Rule says:

When a request comes to myapp.example.com/app (or anything starting with /app),

Rewrite the path to / before sending to the backend service.

So:

http://myapp.example.com/app → backend sees /

http://myapp.example.com/app/v1 → backend also sees /

http://myapp.example.com/app/login → backend still sees /

👉 Everything after /app is dropped because we rewrote the target to /.

🔹 If You Want to Keep the Remaining Path
Instead of always rewriting to /, you can use regex captures.

Example:
annotations:
  nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/rewrite-target: /$1
spec:
  rules:
  - host: myapp.example.com
    http:
      paths:
      - path: /app/(.*)
        pathType: Prefix
        backend:
          service:
            name: myapp-service
            port:
              number: 80
Now:

http://myapp.example.com/app/v1 → backend sees /v1

http://myapp.example.com/app/login → backend sees /login

http://myapp.example.com/app/ → backend sees /

🎯 Summary
nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/rewrite-target: / → strips everything and always sends / to backend.

If you want to preserve part of the path, use regex with /(.*) and $1.

